{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-107412",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+2)(2x-1)}{(x-2)(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne \\pm 2,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=-\\frac{2x-1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=2.$ So statement II is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ | $2$ | $2.001$ | $2.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $298$ | $2\\,998$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-3\\,002$ | $-302$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 2$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 2$ from the right . Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14188718/assets/9e96d6a81813c1c89a498d0206234f2685f0a53c1bab626ce7430d84f9c0f506.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
